  THE READING GUARD THAT MATTERS MOST FOR THIS CLASS
  Three of the five methods declare an expected exception, at source lines 159, 189 and 219,
  each naming the same authentication service exception type. Under the JUnit 4 generation this
  module uses, such a method PASSES when the declared exception is thrown and fails only when
  it is not thrown. Their method names contain the word shouldThrow, which reads like a failure
  and is not one. Each of the three is therefore an ordinary testcase element carrying no
  failure and no error child, exactly like the other two, and the absence of those children for
  them is correct rather than an omission awaiting repair. Attaching a failure element to any of
  the three, on the strength of its method name or of the exception type its annotation names,
  would record authentication failures that no run reported: three fabricated failures in the
  migration's only security adjacent module, and under the rule labelled R-5 here three phantom
  failures with no register row, which is to say three fictitious migration regressions.
  The annotation level occurrence of the expected attribute is confined to those three lines.
  The same word also appears as a local variable name at six further lines, three declarations
  and three uses, so a plain search for the word overstates the annotation count. The brief for
  this file put those further lines at three; the verified figure of six is stated here with the
  plan's noted, rather than the plan's figure being repeated silently.

