  MODULE CONTEXT
  This module contains the migration's only security adjacent source edit, and this folder of
  reports is the module's regression net. Because the module's main source was edited, an
  unregistered failure here is a security outcome regression and is blocking rather than a note,
  and that applies with full force to this report, which covers the authentication decision
  itself. Described neutrally, and without reproducing any audited text, the edit is this: a class
  literal reference to a vendor internal naming factory was replaced by the identical class name
  as a string, and two values were externalized into Spring configuration unchanged. Of the three
  occurrences in that class, only the class literal field initializer is visible to the compiler;
  the other two are a configuration string literal and a Javadoc block. That distinction matters
  because the static audit gate is textual rather than semantic, so a build that compiles, deploys
  and passes every functional flow can still fail it on the two occurrences no compiler ever sees.
  The remediation changes the context factory field from a class type to a string type with a
  matching accessor and mutator and passes the class name at its single functional use, which the
  naming environment consumes identically; it is therefore a representation change and not a
  behaviour change. The asymmetry that makes the fix possible is a property of the platform and
  not a workaround: compiling against the encapsulated class fails, while a runtime lookup of the
  same class by its name succeeds. That is why the register of platform internal access exceptions
  in production launch configuration, referred to by title only, is delivered empty and says so.
  The zero there is earned: every accessibility failure in this migration sat inside a test
  library and was resolved by upgrading or removing that library, every reflective access widening
  call site in the codebase targets the application's own classes, and no platform security
  manager is installed anywhere in the codebase. Those last two points were checked precisely
  rather than by name: the four reflective access widening call sites all widen a field of an
  application class, and the only types in the tree whose names end in security manager belong to
  a third party document sharing library and to the application interface that adapts it, while
  the platform type of that name is installed nowhere.
  This module is the clearest illustration in the whole change set of the transformation rule
  labelled R-T3 here, paraphrased rather than quoted: where a dependency fails under the platform's
  stronger encapsulation the resolution is to upgrade or replace that dependency, and platform
  internal call sites are rewritten to supported APIs rather than accommodated by widening module
